<article class="env-remark" id="091E">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/091E">Remark <span data-tag="091E">15.89.2</span>.</a> This remark is a continuation of Remark <a href="/tag/091A" data-tag="091A">15.88.8</a>. A sheaf of rings on $\mathbf{N}$ is just an inverse system of rings $(A_ n)$. A sheaf of modules over $(A_ n)$ is exactly the same thing as an object of the category $\textit{Mod}(\mathbf{N}, (A_ n))$ defined above. The derived functor $R\mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits $ of Lemma <a href="/tag/091D" data-tag="091D">15.89.1</a> is simply $R\Gamma (\mathbf{N}, -)$ from the derived category of modules to the derived category of modules over the global sections of the structure sheaf. It is true in general that cohomology of groups and modules agree, see Cohomology on Sites, Lemma <a href="/tag/03FD" data-tag="03FD">21.12.4</a>. </p>
</article>